cific utility line marked \"OHE\" running horizontally along the southern (bottom) perimeter of the site.\"\n\nSo there may be TWO OHE markings - one on the west boundary (from Sheet 21) and one on the south boundary (from Sheet 31 landscape plan).\n\n\nThe landscape plan shows utility lines with OHE marked on the south and potentially other utilities elsewhere. The critical issue is that trees are positioned near overhead electric infrastructure, which means I need to verify this against clearance standards. Let me examine the plan blocks for any notes about electrical clearances or spacing requirements.\n\nFrom my review of the sheets, none of the blocks on Sheet 21, Sheet 31, or Sheet 35 (landscape calculations) actually address electrical clearances for trees. This is a significant gap—the clearance requirements outlined in EL-2.1 through EL-2.15 don't appear to have been incorporated into the design documentation.\n\nTo verify compliance, I need to cross-reference the plant species against the UC designation in ECM Appendix F. The species I'm seeing include Cedar Elm, Live Oak, Texas Mountain Laurel, and Mexican Redbud, but I haven't yet confirmed their utility compatibility status or whether they meet the clearance standards for distribution and transmission lines.\n\nI also need to check if any of the proposed trees exceed 40 feet at maturity, verify whether trees are positioned appropriately relative to the three poles and four transformer pads shown on Sheet 21, and confirm the clearance distances from underground equipment and pull boxes. The documentation also needs to address measurement and verification requirements outlined in the later standards.\n\nThe main challenge is that while trees appear on the landscape plan near the overhead electric line, I lack the specific utility compatibility standards from ECM Appendix F and don't see explicit clearance dimensions marked on the plans. I should measure distances between trees and electrical infrastructure using available tools, attempt to determine species compatibility requirements, and identify which compliance items can be verified versus those requiring additional information. Starting with the Sheet 21 vision analysis, I can locate the three utility poles along the western boundary and the transformer pads to establish baseline distances.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f00b2f4f-4bf8-4369-b6e1-683efc1760e1","uuid":"ce41182f-a59a-4af6-b312-ce26df7d2073"}
