ping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structure/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be mountable curbing.\"\n*   \"12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\"\n*   \"13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\"\n\n**3. 48-hour notification requirements for work near electric lines?**\nYes, there is a requirement for a pre-construction meeting 48 hours in advance:\n\nFrom the **Electric Transmission Notes** section:\n*   \"3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (alt 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20’ clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\"\n\n**4. Tree pruning or maintenance near overhead lines?**\nYes, tree pruning, planting, and maintenance are addressed:\n\nFrom the **Electric Notes** section:\n*   \"1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\"\n*   \"3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy’s work within the limits of construction for this project.\"\n\nFrom the **Electric Transmission Notes** section:\n*   \"1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\"\n\n**5. Construction staging areas near overhead electric facilities?**\nYes, there is a specific restriction regarding staging areas:\n\nFrom the **Electric Transmission Notes** section:\n*   \"7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\""}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ce5a084e-a0f7-4302-9557-5dc167da4ff6","uuid":"abc0f0ee-7d6b-4e96-b2fa-fe35b6e44190","tool_use_result":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a careful examination of the provided site plan document, there are several notes addressing your specific questions. Below are the exact quotes found in the **Electric Notes** and **Electric Transmission Notes** sections (Note: The text from the *Electric Notes* section is also duplicated in an unnumbered block of text directly below the *Fire Department* section).\n\n**1. Electric transmission line clearances or safety requirements?**\nYes, there are multiple notes regarding clearances and safety:\n\nFrom the **Electric Transmission Notes** section:\n*   \"2. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures, including down guy anchors. Grading near electric transmission facilities must be coordinated with Austin Energy prior to commencement of grading. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a meeting.\"\n*   \"3. ...OSHA requires a 20’ clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\"\n*   \"4. Barricades must be erected 10 feet from electric transmission structures during construction.\"\n*   \"6. Warning signs must be placed under the overhead electric transmission facilities as notification of the electrical hazard.\"\n*   \"15. Do not dig or grade within 25 feet of the transmission structures (including down guy anchors).\"\n*   \"16. Owner may not place, erect, construct or maintain the following within electric transmission easement:\n    *   any permanent structures, including, but not limited to habitable structures such as homes, mobile homes, garages, or offices.\n    *   any structure of any kind in such proximity to transmission or distribution lines, poles, structures, towers, or appurtenant facilities as would constitute a violation of the National Electric Safety Code in effect at the time the structure is erected...\"\n\nFrom the **Electric Notes** section:\n*   \"4. The owner of the property is responsible for maintaining clearances required by the National Electric Safety Code, Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) regulations, City of Austin rules and regulations and Texas state laws pertaining to clearances when working in close proximity to overhead power lines and equipment. Austin Energy will not render electric service unless required clearances are maintained. All costs incurred because of failure to comply with the required clearances will be charged to the owner.\"\n\n**2. Austin Energy easement requirements or overhead electric facility requirements?**\nYes, these requirements are detailed extensively:\n\nFrom the **Electric Notes** section:\n*   \"2. The owner/developer of this subdivision/lot shall provide Austin Energy with any easement and/or access required, in addition to those indicated, for the installation and ongoing maintenance of overhead and underground electric facilities. These easements and/or access are required to provide electric service to the building and will not be located so as to cause the site to be out of compliance with Chapter 25-8 of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\"\n*   \"5. Any relocation of electric facilities shall be at the landowner’s/developer’s expense.\"\n\nFrom the **Electric Transmission Notes** section:\n*   \"5. Any relocation of electric transmission facilities or outages caused by this project will be charged to the property owner and contractor.\"\n*   \"8. 24 hour access to electric facilities must be maintained.\"\n*   \"9. Any temporary or permanent fence preventing access to the transmission facilities and/or easement shall be coordinated with Austin Energy staff. AE staff will install a lock on the gate for access.\"\n*   \"11. Property owner is responsible for any damages to curbing, landscaping, walls, paving placed around the electric transmission structure/poles/lines caused by Austin Energy during maintenance and repairs. All curbing within the electric transmission easement must be mountable curbing.\"\n*   \"12. Roads/driveways/pavement/parking within the easement should be built to support a 46,000 lb tandem axle (5 foot spacing) to ensure safety.\"\n*   \"13. Fire hydrants must be located out of the transmission easement and a minimum of 20 feet from any transmission structure.\"\n\n**3. 48-hour notification requirements for work near electric lines?**\nYes, there is a requirement for a pre-construction meeting 48 hours in advance:\n\nFrom the **Electric Transmission Notes** section:\n*   \"3. A pre-construction safety meeting is required with Austin Energy 48 hours before commencement of construction. Call Andrew Perez at 512-505-7153 to schedule a tailgate safety meeting. Include Chuck Hendry (alt 505-7151) in the meeting, if cranes are being used during construction. OSHA requires a 20’ clearance from energized transmission lines during construction.\"\n\n**4. Tree pruning or maintenance near overhead lines?**\nYes, tree pruning, planting, and maintenance are addressed:\n\nFrom the **Electric Notes** section:\n*   \"1. Austin Energy has the right to prune and/or remove trees, shrubbery and other obstructions to the extent necessary to keep the easements clear. Austin Energy will perform all tree work in compliance with Chapter 25-8, Subchapter B of the City of Austin Land Development Code.\"\n*   \"3. The owner shall be responsible for installation of temporary erosion control, revegetation and tree protection. In addition, the owner shall be responsible for any initial tree pruning and tree removal that is within ten feet of the center line of the proposed overhead electric facilities designed to provide electric service to this project. The owner shall include Austin Energy’s work within the limits of construction for this project.\"\n\nFrom the **Electric Transmission Notes** section:\n*   \"1. No trees shall be planted within the transmission easement. No trees shall be planted within 25 feet of the base of the transmission structure. Vehicular access for Austin Energy trucks and equipment is always to be maintained within the easement. Austin Energy will not be responsible for damage and/or removal of vegetation within the easement.\"\n\n**5. Construction staging areas near overhead electric facilities?**\nYes, there is a specific restriction regarding staging areas:\n\nFrom the **Electric Transmission Notes** section:\n*   \"7. For safety reasons, aerial equipment, dumpsters, staging or spoils areas are not permitted within 20 feet of the transmission wire and/or structures and must be located outside the easement.\""}]}
{"level":30,"time":1778188796961,"pid":123,"hostname":"082c71e6-af9","step":"review-runs","item":"1.md","index":9,"runIndex":"run-3","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR24Z7CJZ7JCB0W56KD32AN7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my comprehensive research of the site plans, I now have sufficient evidence to evaluate the checklist items. Let me compile my findings.\n\n## RESEARCH SUMMARY\n\n**Overhead Electric Infrastructure Present:**\n- Overhead Electric (OHE) line runs along the southern property boundary (east-west direction)\n- At least 3 utility poles visible on the site plan\n- Guy wire extends from southeastern pole\n- 5-foot electrical easements indicate distribution-level infrastructure\n- Buildings 7 and 8 are the closest structures to this line\n\n**Critical Missing Elements:**\n- **NO clearance dimension annotations** on any plan sheet\n- **NO surveyed conductor elevations** shown on building elevations\n- **NO section views** demonstrating required clearances\n- **NO registered surveyor data** for vertical clearance verification\n- **NO building elevations** with conductor location marked\n- All clearance verification required by UCM § 1.10.3 is absent\n\n**Plan Sheet Coverage:**\n- Sheet 9 (Site Plan): Shows OHE line and buildings but no clearance dimensions\n- Sheet 21 (Electrical Design): Shows only underground electrical infrastructure\n- Sheet 31 (Landscape Plan): Shows OHE line with no clearance annotations\n- Sheet 11 (Grading Plan): Shows electrical easements only, no overhead routing\n- No building elevation sheets or section views showing overhead line clearances\n\n**Austin Energy Requirements (Sheet 3):**\n- Owner responsible for maintaining clearances per NESC, OSHA, City rules, and Texas law\n- Austin Energy will NOT render service unless clearances are maintained\n- Owner responsible for costs of non-compliance\n\nNow let me evaluate the checklist items:"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3251e5e3-3f03-4e85-9bab-d2e9039fdd51","uuid":"c5b9b8cc-e34c-4262-8560-3565e5bb1313"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188802417,"pid":123,"hostname":"082c71e6-af9","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR24YXN6DYSHKV7B2SZZYG52","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is excellent detail about the City Arborist review, but it focuses on tree preservation and mitigation requirements, not specifically on utility clearances. However, I notice that CA15 mentions \"All trees selected must be suitable for the environment of the immediate planting site (see ECM Appendix F for specific categorization such as tree, soil, site, or regulatory qualities).\"\n\nThis is important because ECM Appendix F includes the utility compatibility designation. So the arborist review does implicitly require that trees be suitable for utility clearances.\n\nNow I need to focus on my specific grouping which is about tree clearances from overhead electric lines. The key issues I've identified so far are:\n\n1. The landscape plan (Sheet 31) shows mitigation trees (marked with \"M\") along the southern property boundary immediately adjacent to the OHE (overhead electric) line\n\n2. The plant schedule (Sheet 35) doesn't specify what species these mitigation trees are\n\n3. The proposed trees according to Sheet 35 are:\n   - 2 Live Oak\n   - 10 Cedar Elm\n   - 2 Texas Mountain Laurel  - 6 Mexican Redbud\n\n4. The vision analysis showed that tree canopies appear to \"physically overlap\" the OHE line on the drawing at a scale of 1\"=20'\n\n5. No clearance measurements or dimensions are shown on either the electrical plan or landscape plan\n\n6. There's a reference to the tree list on Sheet 32, but that shows EXISTING trees to be retained/removed, not new plantings\n\n7. The electric review requirements reference UCM § 1.10.10.3 & 4 regarding trees\n\nNow I need to evaluate each checklist item. But I'm faced with a critical gap: I don't know the species of the mitigation trees being planted. The plant schedule lists some trees, but doesn't indicate which ones are in which locations.\n\nActually, looking back at the landscape plan vision analysis, it mentioned that the trees along various boundaries had different labels:\n\n\n- Western Boundary (Street Yard along Valley View Road): \"SY/M\" or \"P\"\n- Northern Boundary: \"M\" (mitigation)\n- Interior Parking Islands: \"PL/M\"\n- Eastern Boundary: \"M\"\n- Southern Boundary (Near OHE): \"M\" (three trees)\n\nSo the mitigation trees are distributed around the site, not just the three on the southern boundary. The vision analysis said there are 3 mitigation trees on the southern boundary, but there are more mitigation trees elsewhere. I need to determine whether all the proposed trees on the landscape plan correspond to the species listed in the plant schedule, or if there's a mismatch between what's shown and what's specified.\n\nLooking at the plant schedule, I see 10 Cedar Elm, 2 Live Oak, 2 Texas Mountain Laurel, and 6 Mexican Redbud—totaling 20 trees, which aligns with the vision analysis count. The ornamental species (Texas Mountain Laurel and Mexican Redbud) could be serving multiple functions, including mitigation roles, while some shade trees might also be designated as mitigation. Now I should verify whether these species are appropriate for planting near overhead electric utilities.\n\nThe utility compatibility requirements specify trees should generally be ≤ 20 feet at maturity, with Texas Mountain Laurel listed as an exception at 25 feet. I don't have direct access to ECM Appendix F to verify the other species, which is a gap I need to flag. Based on the clearance standards, non-utility-compatible trees need 15 feet of distribution clearance and 50 feet of transmission clearance, while large trees (40+ feet) require 25 feet and 50 feet respectively. The species on this plan don't appear to exceed the 40-foot threshold for \"large trees,\" though Live Oak typically reaches 40-50 feet, which warrants closer examination.\n\nThe plant schedule shows 16-foot minimum container heights at installation, not mature heights. Looking at typical maturity ranges—Live Oak and Cedar Elm both reaching 40-50 feet, Texas Mountain Laurel at 25 feet, and Mexican Redbud at 25-30 feet—Live Oak and Cedar Elm would qualify as large trees requiring the 25-foot distribution clearance. I need to verify these mature heights against the ECM Appendix F specifications to properly assess compliance with the clearance requirements.\n\nFor the checklist items: I can't determine utility-compatibility status without the appendix data, so EL-2.1 is indeterminate. The OHE line appears to be distribution rather than transmission, making EL-2.2 and EL-2.4 likely inapplicable. However, EL-2.3 presents a potential issue—if Live Oak and Cedar Elm are indeed large trees at 40+ feet mature height, and the vision analysis places them 10-15 feet from the line, this would violate the 25-foot clearance requirement.\n\nLooking at the remaining standards: No transmission easement appears on the landscape plan, so EL-2.5 doesn't apply. For EL-2.6, while poles are shown on the electrical plan, I can't verify specific distances to proposed trees without clearer callouts. Similarly, EL-2.7 requires checking the three interior pad-mounted transformers against tree locations—the data isn't sufficient to confirm compliance. EL-2.8 involves underground lines and pull boxes, but I lack the measurements needed to assess the 5-foot setback requirement.\n\nRoot barrier specifications aren't evident on the landscape plan for EL-2.9. The plant legend identifies mitigation trees only as \"M\" without species detail, and while Sheet 35 provides a schedule, it doesn't clearly link specific species to the mitigation trees shown—this appears to be a failure or unverifiable. Large trees aren't differentiated by size on the landscape plan, which is likely non-compliant. Finally, no clearance measurements are documented on the plans, making EL-2.12 impossible to verify.\n\nFor EL-2.13, the same measurement issue applies—without documented clearances, I can't assess whether they're measured from canopy or trunk. EL-2.14 shows trees and utilities both depicted on the landscape plan, but there are no dimensions or callouts establishing their relationship; the vision analysis suggests canopy overlap with the overhead line, indicating likely non-compliance or inability to verify. Regarding EL-2.15, I don't find Austin Energy approval documentation in the master comment report—the electric review references requirements but doesn't demonstrate approval was actually obtained, so this appears to be a failure or unverifiable condition.\n\nLooking more carefully at what's actually documented: the landscape plan includes trees positioned near the OHE line, yet the plant schedule fails to identify which species correspond to which locations on the plan. No clearance dimensions appear anywhere on the drawings. The specific trees shown along the southern boundary adjacent to the overhead line aren't identified by species, which directly contradicts the requirement for a complete plant legend. This represents a clear deficiency for EL-2.10 and raises questions about EL-2.14's compliance with proper utility coordination standards.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7c5d1be6-7d35-4fa2-8909-ba4ff47d7c41","uuid":"5bff5961-639e-4d95-b827-215d71407166"}
{"level":30,"time":1778188803105,"pid":123,"hostname":"082c71e6-af9","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":1,"runIndex":"run-1","documentId":"1144b126-1216-4dee-a89c-178950fa7777","sheetNum":31,"prompt":"Examine the landscape plan carefully and identify tree species. Look for any text labels, callouts, or notations that identify specific tree species at or near: (1) the southwest corner tree(s); (2) the three southeast corner mitigation trees; (3) the row of five trees along the western boundary in the street yard area. Also check if the plant schedule from Sheet 35 cross-references specific locations on this landscape plan, and determine which of the proposed species (Live Oak, Cedar Elm, Mexican Redbud, Texas Mountain Laurel) are located where. Identify any species labels next to the tree symbols.","res":{"steps":[{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on a careful visual analysis of the provided landscape plan (Sheet 28), specific tree species are **not** identified anywhere on this document. The tree symbols on this plan are only labeled with abbreviations indicating their regulatory landscape category, not their botanical species. \n\nHere is the detailed breakdown of your requested areas based exactly on what is written on the plan:\n\n**(1) Southwest corner tree(s):**\nThere are tree symbols located near the southwest corner of the site. However, there are no text labels, callouts, or notations indicating their specific species. Nearby text includes labels like \"PL\" and \"M\". \n\n**(2) Three southeast corner mitigation trees:**\nIn the southeast corner of the property line, there are three distinct tree symbols. \n*   **Exact Labels Found:** Each of these three tree symbols is labeled with the letter **\"M\"**. \n*   According to the legend in the top right under \"KEY NOTES\", **\"M\"** stands for **\"M - MITIGATION TREE\"**. \n*   No species names are listed.\n\n**(3) Row of five trees along the western boundary in the street yard area:**\nAlong the western boundary (adjacent to the street), inside the shaded area designated as \"H-2\" (STREET YARD), there is a row of five trees.\n*   **Exact Labels Found:** Each of these five tree symbols has a small text label next to it that reads **\"ST\"**. \n*   According to the legend, **\"ST\"** stands for **\"ST - STREET YARD TREE (EXCESS OF 1.5 CAL. INCH COUNTING TOWARDS MITIGATION)\"**.\n*   No species names are listed.\n\n**Regarding Sheet 35 and specific species (Live Oak, Cedar Elm, Mexican Redbud, Texas Mountain Laurel):**\n*   This document is explicitly labeled \"Sheet 28 of 35\". Sheet 35 is not included in this view.\n*   On this specific sheet (Sheet 28), there are **no cross-references** to a plant schedule on Sheet 35 that link specific locations to species. \n*   The terms \"Live Oak\", \"Cedar Elm\", \"Mexican Redbud\", and \"Texas Mountain Laurel\" do not appear anywhere in the text, notes, or labels on this page. \n*   All tree symbols are strictly labeled by their function/category (**ST**, **PL**, **P**, **M**) as defined in the key notes, rather than by species.","providerMetadata":{"google":{"thoughtSignature":""}}}],"finishReason":"stop","usage":{"inputTokens":1437,"outputTokens":532,"totalTokens":4234,"reasoningTokens":2265},"warnings":[],"request":{"body":{"prompt":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"file","mediaType":"image/jpeg","data":"[BINARY_DATA_REMOVED - length: 1755680]"},{"type":"text","text":"# Your task\n\nYou are analyzing a subset of a real estate development site plan submission document.\n\nVisually analyze the binary file provided with extreme accuracy. Pay close attention to tables, reproducing them EXACTLY as they appear, character-by-character.\n\nCRITICAL: Do not paraphrase, interpret, or \"fill in\" text. If you cannot read something clearly, state \"text unclear\" rather than guessing. Transcribe field labels and values exactly as written.\n\nIf the requirements being validated require certain content to be present, look closely for it and provide all relevant information that pertains to the broader request. Remember that you are only looking at a single supplementary document, or a single page from the primary site plan. Just because you do not find evidence for a requirement on this page does not mean it will not be present on another page.\n\nReport back precisely on what you see, using exact quotes for any text you transcribe from forms, tables, or labels.\n\nThe final review agent will not be able to look at this document directly, so you must be their eyes. Ensure you describe everything relevant in full detail with perfect accuracy.\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Examine the landscape plan carefully and identify tree species. Look for any text labels, callouts, or notations that identify specific tree species at or near: (1) the southwest corner tree(s); (2) the three southeast corner mitigation trees; (3) the row of five trees along the western boundary in the street yard area. Also check if the plant schedule from Sheet 35 cross-references specific locations on this landscape plan, and determine which of the proposed species (Live Oak, Cedar Elm, Mexican Redbud, Texas Mountain Laurel) are located where. Identify any species labels next to the tree symbols."}]}],"providerOptions":{"gateway":{"user":"b877dead-f786-4bf6-9ac4-3cde3f2ec546","tags":["label:VISION_CHECK_REVIEW_EL_MD_EXP_VAR1_RUN_1","tool:vision"]}},"headers":{"user-agent":"ai/5.0.152"}}},"response":{"id":"aitxt-fMzVxMj5HktEExDNNYy9YY0r","timestamp":{},"modelId":"google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview","headers":{"access-control-allow-headers":"Content-Type, Authorization, ai-gateway-auth-method, ai-gateway-protocol-version, ai-model-id, ai-language-model-id, ai-language-model-specification-version, ai-image-model-specification-version, ai-embedding-model-specification-version, ai-language-model-streaming, ai-reporting-tags, ai-reporting-user, http-referer, x-title, anthropic-beta","access-control-allow-methods":"GET, POST, P