{"level":30,"time":1778189049857,"pid":123,"hostname":"082c71e6-af9","step":"review-runs","item":"2.md","index":4,"runIndex":"run-2","retry":1,"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR256JGTVG0NTW73P4R0DYK5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The measure-distance tool failed because it couldn't resolve the plan set version for the document ID. This is a technical issue with the tool's access to the document versioning system.\n\nGiven this limitation, I need to work with the information I have from the vision analysis and the plans themselves. Let me proceed with my evaluation based on the evidence I've gathered:\n\n**What I can determine:**\n\n1. **From Vision Analysis of Sheet 31:** The trees along the southern boundary are \"directly on top of, or immediately adjacent to, the line labeled '- OHE -'. The drawn canopies heavily overlap the OHE line.\"\n\n2. **This indicates:** The trees are within extremely close proximity to the OHE line - likely within 0-5 feet based on the visual representation\n\n3. **Clearance Requirements per Grouping File (UCM § 1.10.10.4):**\n   - Non-utility-compatible trees: Must be ≥ 15 feet from distribution conductors\n   - Large trees (40+ feet): Must be ≥ 25 feet from distribution conductors\n   - Utility-compatible trees: May be within 15 feet\n\n4. **Key Finding:** The landscape plan shows no designation of tree utility compatibility, no clearance dimensions, and no evidence of Austin Energy approval for any exceptions.\n\nNow let me evaluate each checklist item:\n\n**EL-2.1:** Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 15 lateral feet of overhead electric distribution conductor\n- The OHE line appears to be a distribution line (typical voltage ~12.47kV or 34.5kV)\n- Trees shown on Sheet 31 are adjacent to or overlapping the OHE line\n- The plant schedule does NOT identify which trees are utility-compatible\n- WITHOUT specification of utility compatibility AND WITH trees shown adjacent to the line, this appears to be a FAIL or NOT-VERIFIABLE situation\n\n**EL-2.2:** Non-utility-compatible trees proposed within 50 lateral feet of overhead electric transmission conductor\n\n\n- Facts.md indicates this site is served by distribution, not transmission lines, so the OHE line is distribution-level and this item appears NOT-APPLICABLE\n\n**EL-2.3:** Large trees (40+ feet mature) proposed within 25 lateral feet of distribution\n- The plans show Live Oak and Cedar Elm at 16' planting size, both capable of reaching 40+ feet at maturity, but documented mature heights per ECM Appendix F are not provided, making this NOT-VERIFIABLE\n\n**EL-2.4:** Large trees within 50 lateral feet of transmission\n- NOT-APPLICABLE since the site has no transmission lines\n\n**EL-2.5:** Trees proposed within transmission easement boundaries\n- NOT-APPLICABLE; Facts.md only references distribution electrical easements (5' width), not transmission easements\n\n**EL-2.6:** Trees within 10 lateral feet of overhead utility pole\n- Sheet 31 shows OHE poles with trees positioned nearby, but specific pole locations aren't measured, so this is NOT-VERIFIABLE\n\n**EL-2.7:** Trees within 10 lateral feet of pad-mounted equipment\n- Vision analysis identified a tree tagged \"SY\" in the mid-west section of the shaded Street Yard drawn extremely close to the rectangular electrical pad at the terminus of the \"- E -\" line, with the tree canopy overlapping the pad itself. This indicates either a FAIL or NOT-VERIFIABLE condition.\n\n**EL-2.8:** Trees within 5 lateral feet of underground electric equipment\n- Underground electric lines are shown on Sheet 31, but tree distances from these lines aren't specified, making this NOT-VERIFIABLE\n\n**EL-2.9:** Trees within 20 lateral feet of underground equipment lack root barriers\n- Root barriers (4' deep, 5' from equipment) would be required if trees are within 20 feet, but none are shown on the landscape plans, resulting in NOT-VERIFIABLE or FAIL\n\n**EL-2.10:** Plant legend or schedule doesn't identify species\n- Sheet 35 provides a plant schedule listing both common and botanical names for all species\n\n, so this appears to PASS\n\n**EL-2.11:** Large trees not differentiated on plans\n- While the plant schedule identifies species, it doesn't note mature heights per ECM Appendix F, and trees aren't marked as \"large\" or \"non-large\" on the landscape plan itself, making this NOT-VERIFIABLE or FAIL\n\n**EL-2.12 & EL-2.13:** Clearances measured incorrectly\n- No clearances are dimensioned anywhere on the landscape plans, so both of these are NOT-VERIFIABLE\n\n**EL-2.14:** Trees not shown in relation to overhead electric facilities\n- The landscape plan does show the OHE line with trees positioned along it, but the relationship between them remains unclear because no clearance dimensions are provided. Trees appear to overlap the OHE line visually, which suggests a FAIL for lack of compliance documentation.\n\n**EL-2.15:** No Austin Energy written approval for trees in prohibited zones\n- There's no documented Austin Energy written approval on the plans, and trees appear within prohibited zones adjacent to the OHE line, indicating a FAIL.\n\nHowever, I need to verify whether the southern boundary OHE line actually falls outside the property boundary in the street right-of-way, which could change the applicability of these requirements.\n\nThe vision analysis indicates the OHE line runs parallel to the property line and Limits of Construction along the southern edge. If the OHE line is positioned outside the LOC in the public right-of-way, then trees planted inside the LOC on private property wouldn't necessarily violate clearance requirements. But the analysis also notes that proposed mitigation trees and existing trees along the southern boundary are drawn directly on or immediately adjacent to the OHE line with canopies heavily overlapping it, which creates ambiguity about whether the trees actually intrude into the OHE clearance zone.\n\nI need to work with the assumption that the OHE line sits outside the property boundary, the canopy circles represent mature spread, and any overlap between those canopies and the OHE line indicates a potential clearance violation. The planning documents should demonstrate adequate distance from tree trunk centers to the outside conductor.\n\nThis leads to several compliance gaps: the trees lack utility compatibility designations, and there appear to be clearance conflicts that need resolution.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed658f06-b65b-479d-b752-f47b92243df6","uuid":"75f249b7-6d9b-45b5-96f0-295a7e313b4b"}
