{"level":30,"time":1778189160447,"pid":123,"hostname":"082c71e6-af9","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"13.json","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PVSQ51PscQSvAJptoGjQdW","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Site Plan Facts\n     2→\n     3→# Facts — Valley View Townhomes (SP-2025-0126C)\n     4→**3759 Valley View Road, Austin, TX 78704**\n     5→\n     6→---\n     7→\n     8→## Discrepancies\n     9→\n    10→- **Easement (Greenbelt Access & Use, Instrument 2010070731):** A perpetual, no-build greenbelt easement covering 0.31 acres (13,524 sq ft) of the NE interior of Lot 1 is NOT depicted on the submitted site plan and is NOT in the COA GIS easement layer. County clerk research confirms it is real, recorded, and perpetual. Must be depicted before approval.\n    11→- **Easement (City of Austin Drainage, Instrument 2011021137):** A perpetual City of Austin drainage easement covering 0.130 acres (5,670 sq ft) of the NE corner of Lot 1 is NOT depicted on the submitted site plan and is NOT in the COA GIS easement layer. County clerk research confirms it is real, recorded, and perpetual. Must be depicted before approval.\n    12→- **Combined NE no-build zone (~0.44 ac):** Together, the two easements above encumber approximately 0.44 acres (~19%) of the NE portion of the 2.16-acre site. Neither is shown on submitted plans. Proposed building layout must be evaluated against both easement footprints.\n    13→- **Acreage:** Site plan states 2.16 ac; 2019 deed/survey states 2.158 ac; TCAD states 2.1970 ac; COA GIS parcel measures 2.111 ac (91,944 sq ft). If the controlling acreage is 2.111 ac (GIS), the IC calculation based on 2.16 ac should be rechecked.\n    14→- **10' Wastewater Easement (Vol 73/76, C8S-75-160):** COA GIS shows this plat-dedicated easement running across the parcel. Not confirmed on the submitted site plan. Site plan notes \"must verify no conflicts.\"\n    15→- **25' Drainage Easement and 7.5' PUE (Vol 64/13, C8-71-079):** GIS shows these easements (from the Cortez Heights plat) running across the NE boundary of Lot 1. Not verified on site plan. Precise location relative to proposed improvements must be confirmed.\n    16→- **FIRM Panel Number:** Site plan cites Panel #48453C0585H (effective September 26, 2008). FloodPro returns null for both FIRM_PANEL and EFFECTIVE_DATE. Must be independently verified on FEMA Flood Map Service Center.\n    17→- **Demolition permit number:** Site plan cites \"2022-101266 BP\" as the demolition permit. Amanda shows 2022-101266 as a Plumbing Permit (Repair); the actual demolition building permit is 2022-096813 BP (Final). Actual demolition is confirmed complete.\n    18→\n    19→---\n    20→\n    21→## Property\n    22→\n    23→- **Address:** 3759 Valley View Road, Austin, TX 78704\n    24→- **Parcel ID (GeoID):** 0406100252\n    25→- **Legal Description:** Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas\n    26→- **Subdivision:** Mecey Subdivision (File No. C8S-75-100)\n    27→- **Plat Instrument:** Vol 73 / Pg 76 (Instrument 418356), filed 1975-12-11\n    28→- **County:** Travis\n    29→- **Site Area (site plan):** 2.16 acres\n    30→- **Site Area (2019 deed/survey):** 2.158 acres\n    31→- **Site Area (TCAD):** 2.1970 acres (95,701 sq ft)\n    32→- **Site Area (COA GIS parcel):** 2.111 acres (91,944 sq ft)\n    33→- **Limits of Construction:** 2.26 acres\n    34→- **Survey of Record (likely):** 2018 CDS Survey, Project No. 18-03-0225:021\n    35→- **Current Land Use:** Vacant land (formerly assisted living / nursing care facility; demolished June 2022)\n    36→\n    37→## Ownership\n    38→\n    39→- **Owner:** WM 41 Austin RE, LLC\n    40→- **Owner Mailing Address:** 500 West 5th Street, Ste. 1010, Austin, TX 78701\n    41→- **Acquisition Deed:** Instrument 2019197883 — Special Warranty Deed from SHG Resources LP to WM 41 Austin RE LLC (2019-12-11). Property conveyed subject to all easements of record.\n    42→- **Civil Engineering Firm:** Bleyl Engineering\n    43→- **Architecture Firm:** Danze & Davis Architects, Inc.\n    44→- **Landscape Architecture Firm:** Blu Fish Collaborative, Inc.\n    45→\n    46→## Zoning & Land Use\n    47→\n    48→- **Zoning:** MF-2 (Multi-Family Residence — Medium Density)\n    49→- **Controlling Ordinance:** 20120112-092 (Zoning Case C14-2011-0093)\n    50→- **Secondary Ordinance:** 19990225-070b\n    51→- **Neighborhood Plan:** South Lamar NPA — SUSPENDED. No active neighborhood plan applies.\n    52→- **Regulating Plan:** None\n    53→- **Historic District / Landmark:** None\n    54→- **Compatibility Standards:** LDC Subchapter F triggered by adjacent SF-3 parcel (0406100210). Within 25 ft of the SF-3 lot line: maximum 2 stories / 30 ft height. Step-up schedule applies for structures beyond 25 ft. Screening/buffer requirements between MF and SF-3 uses apply.\n    55→- **Nonconforming Signs Area:** Yes (§25-10-152) — all signage must comply with nonconforming signs area standards\n    56→- **Parkland Deficient Area:** Yes (2 polygon features intersect parcel) — parkland dedication fee-in-lieu will apply per unit; confirm fee with Parks Department\n    57→- **Residential Design Standards (Subchapter F):** Active overlay\n    58→\n    59→## Flood\n    60→\n    61→- **FEMA Flood Zone:** Zone X (outside 100-year and 500-year floodplain)\n    62→- **COA Flood Zone:** Zone X\n    63→- **FIRM Panel (site plan claim):** 48453C0585H, effective September 26, 2008 — ⚠️ unverifiable in FloodPro; verify on FEMA portal\n    64→- **FIRM Community:** 480624\n    65→- **Elevation Certificate:** None on file\n    66→- **LOMAs on file:** 0\n    67→- **LOMRs on file:** 0\n    68→- **Floodplain intersection:** None (fema-floodplain layer: empty; fully-developed-floodplain layer: empty)\n    69→\n    70→## Watershed & Environmental\n    71→\n    72→- **Watershed:** West Bouldin Creek (WBO) — Watershed ID 109\n    73→- **Receiving Waters:** Lady Bird Lake → Colorado River below Longhorn Dam\n    74→- **Watershed Class:** Urban\n    75→- **Development Zone:** Desired Development Zone\n    76→- **Impervious Cover Cap:** 65% (LDC §25-8-341)\n    77→- **Proposed Impervious Cover:** ~59.5–60% (under cap — calculated against 2.16 ac site area; see acreage discrepancy)\n    78→- **Existing Impervious Cover:** ~65%\n    79→- **RSMP Participation:** No\n    80→- **Smart Growth Zone:** Yes\n    81→- **Drinking Water Protection Zone:** No\n    82→- **Edwards Aquifer Recharge Zone:** No\n    83→- **Edwards Aquifer Contributing Zone:** No\n    84→- **Creek Buffers:** None intersecting parcel\n    85→- **Salamander Habitat:** No\n    86→- **Underground Storage Tanks:** None registered\n    87→- **Wildland Urban Interface (2024):** No\n    88→\n    89→## Easements & Encumbrances\n    90→\n    91→### ⛔ Critical — Not Depicted on Site Plan, Not in COA GIS\n    92→\n    93→**Easement 1 — Greenbelt Access & Use Easement**\n    94→- **Instrument:** 2010070731 (filed 2010-05-18; executed 2010-03-12)\n    95→- **Grantor:** SHG Resources LP | **Grantee:** Dupont / Barnett / Roberts (assignable to HOA)\n    96→- **Area:** 0.31 acres (13,524 sq ft), NE interior of Lot 1\n    97→- **Duration:** PERPETUAL\n    98→- **Restrictions:** No-build zone. Exclusive pedestrian/greenbelt open space for adjacent condo project. No structures or improvements permitted in easement area.\n    99→- **Status on Site Plan:** Not depicted — must be added before approval\n   100→- **Status in COA GIS:** Not present in easement-polygons layer\n   101→\n   102→**Easement 2 — City of Austin Drainage Easement**\n   103→- **Instrument:** 2011021137 (filed 2011-02-11; executed 2010-03-15)\n   104→- **Grantor:** SHG Resources LP | **Grantee:** City of Austin (Watershed Protection)\n   105→- **Area:** 0.130 acres (5,670 sq ft), NE corner of Lot 1\n   106→- **Duration:** PERPETUAL\n   107→- **Restrictions:** City holds unlimited rights to construct, operate, maintain, replace, upgrade, and repair a drainage channel. No development may occur without City of Austin consent. Related project: Clawson MultiFamily, SP-2010-0014C.SH.\n   108→- **Status on Site Plan:** Not depicted — must be added before approval; requires Watershed Protection coordination\n   109→- **Status in COA GIS:** Not present in easement-polygons layer\n   110→\n   111→### ⚠️ GIS-Confirmed — Not Verified on Site Plan\n   112→\n   113→**Easement 3 — 10' Wastewater Easement**\n   114→- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 73/76, Case C8S-75-160 (Mecey Subdivision plat)\n   115→- **Width:** 10 ft\n   116→- **Status:** Plat-dedicated. In COA GIS. Not confirmed depicted on site plan. Site plan notes \"must verify no conflicts.\"\n   117→\n   118→**Easement 4 — 25' Drainage Easement (from Cortez Heights plat)**\n   119→- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 64/13, Case C8-71-079\n   120→- **Width:** 25 ft\n   121→- **Status:** DEDICATED. Runs across NE boundary of Lot 1. Not verified on site plan.\n   122→\n   123→**Easement 5 — 7.5' Public Utility Easement (from Cortez Heights plat)**\n   124→- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 64/13, Case C8-71-079\n   125→- **Width:** 7.5 ft\n   126→- **Status:** DEDICATED. Runs across NE boundary of Lot 1. Not verified on site plan.\n   127→\n   128→**Easement 6 — 10' Drainage Easement (from Trico Subd plat)**\n   129→- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 69/14, Case C8S-74-075\n   130→- **Width:** 10 ft\n   131→- **Status:** DEDICATED. Runs across NE boundary of Lot 1. Not verified on site plan.\n   132→\n   133→### Plat-Dedicated (Confirm on Survey)\n   134→\n   135→**Easement 7 & 8 — 5' Electric Easements (×2)**\n   136→- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 73/76, Case C8S-75-160\n   137→- **Width:** 5 ft each\n   138→- **Status:** DEDICATED\n   139→\n   140→**Easement 9 — 7.5' Public Utility Easement**\n   141→- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 73/76, Case C8S-75-160\n   142→- **Width:** 7.5 ft\n   143→- **Status:** DEDICATED\n   144→\n   145→### Recorded Document Provisions\n   146→\n   147→**Instrument 2010070731 — Greenbelt Access & Use Easement:**\n   148→- Entire 0.31-acre NE interior area is a strict no-build zone; no structures, fences, pavement, or permanent improvements permitted\n   149→- Easement is exclusive to the adjacent condo project beneficiaries and is freely assignable to the project's HOA\n   150→- Runs with the land; binds all successors and assigns of the grantor\n   151→- No time limit; perpetual duration\n   152→- Current property owner (WM 41) took title subject to this easement per the 2019 acquisition deed\n   153→\n   154→**Instrument 2011021137 — City of Austin Drainage Easement:**\n   155→- City of Austin (Watershed Protection Department) holds unlimited, irrevocable right of access to the 0.130-acre NE corner area\n   156→- City may construct, reconstruct, operate, inspect, maintain, repair, replace, and upgrade a drainage channel within the easement\n   157→- Owner may not obstruct City access; any improvements within or adjacent to the easement require City coordination\n   158→- Perpetual; binds all successors and assigns\n   159→\n   160→**Deed Restrictions / Covenants:** None found. County clerk search of 10 strategies found no height limits, use restrictions, parking ratios, setback covenants, or design review obligations recorded against Lot 1.\n   161→\n   162→## Jurisdiction\n   163→\n   164→- **Jurisdiction:** City of Austin — Full Purpose (Code 210)\n   165→- **County:** Travis\n   166→- **Council District:** 5\n   167→- **School District:** Austin ISD (AISD)\n   168→\n   169→## Roadway Classification\n   170→\n   171→**Valley View Road**\n   172→- **ASMP Street Level:** 1 — Local Street\n   173→- **AMTP Classification:** Not in AMTP (local street — not classified as highway, arterial, expressway, or freeway)\n   174→- **Project Type:** Local Mobility\n   175→- **Segment Limits:** Manchaca Rd to End\n   176→- **Existing Lane Count:** Not on file\n   177→- **Future Cross-Section:** 2U-OP (2 undivided lanes, open pavement)\n   178→- **Required ROW:** 58 or 64 ft (determination required — this is commercial redevelopment; single-family exception does not apply)\n   179→- **Required Improvement:** Complete missing sidewalks within existing ROW\n   180→- **Bicycle Facility:** None (existing and recommended)\n   181→- **ROW Dedication Note:** ASMP remarks state ROW dedication may be required from new development and commercial redevelopment through the land development process. Reviewer must determine applicable standard (58 or 64 ft).\n   182→\n   183→## Adjacent Properties\n   184→\n   185→- **SF-3 parcel (0406100210):** Immediate neighbor — triggers LDC Subchapter F compatibility standards. This is the critical adjacency: multi-family development within 25 ft of this SF-3 lot line is subject to 2-story / 30-ft height cap and step-up schedule.\n   186→- **GO parcel (0406100168):** General Office — adjacent\n   187→- **MF-3 parcel (0406100170):** Multi-Family Medium-High Density — adjacent\n   188→- **MF-3-CO parcel (0404090425):** Multi-Family Medium-High Density with Conditional Overlay — adjacent\n   189→- **MF-2-CO parcel (0406101601):** Multi-Family Medium Density with Conditional Overlay — adjacent\n   190→\n   191→The surrounding context is predominantly multi-family and office, consistent with infill development. The single SF-3 parcel is the critical zoning transition requiring Subchapter F compliance.\n   192→\n   193→## Site Plan Details\n   194→\n   195→- **Site Plan Number:** SP-2025-0126C\n   196→- **Submittal Date:** May 20, 2025\n   197→- **Status:** Awaiting Update (first-round comment response pending)\n   198→- **Fiscal Surety Records:** Filed 2025-09-21 (one for SP-2025-0126C, one for Erosion Controls) — both \"New\" status; suggests progression toward pre-construction conditions\n   199→- **Active Tree Permit:** Commercial Tree Permit pending (filed 2025-08-14)\n   200→\n   201→## Development Specifications\n   202→\n   203→- **Project Name:** Valley View Townhomes\n   204→- **Proposed Use:** 39 multi-family townhome units, 8 buildings\n   205→- **Occupancy Classification:** R3\n   206→- **Construction Type:** Type V-B\n   207→- **Sprinkler System:** NFPA 13D\n   208→- **Proposed Impervious Cover:** ~59.5–60% (reduction from existing ~65%)\n   209→- **Impervious Cover Cap:** 65% (Urban watershed)\n   210→\n   211→## Stormwater\n   212→\n   213→- **Stormwater Feature:** Stacked biofiltration / detention pond (privately maintained)\n   214→- **Watershed:** West Bouldin Creek — Urban, Desired Development Zone\n   215→- **IC Cap:** 65% | Proposed IC: ~59.5–60%\n   216→\n   217→## Fire & Safety\n   218→\n   219→- **Private Hydrants:** 1\n   220→- **Fire Flow Demand:** 3,000 GPM @ 20 psi (reduced to 1,500 GPM with NFPA 13D sprinkler system)\n   221→- **Building Fire Area:** 13,344 sq ft\n   222→- **Wildland Urban Interface:** No\n   223→\n   224→## Development History\n   225→\n   226→- **DA-2021-1307:** Development Assistance, Site Plan Determination/Exemption — Agreed (2021-08-31)\n   227→- **2022-096813 BP:** Building Permit — Demolition — Final (2022-06-28). Existing assisted living building demolished June 2022. Lot currently vacant.\n   228→- **SP-97-0145CS:** Prior site plan — Southwood Nursing Care Center Addition — Approved 1997, Closed 1999 (confirmed prior assisted living use)\n   229→\n   230→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"420720f1-c8ef-4630-b41e-df044b1b2b62","uuid":"ddfec4c3-95e1-4aa5-b7f5-3b33dbeec6f9","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/vercel/sandbox/workspace/projects/63cead15-41f8-418c-b0ef-bd5c2b44719a/facts.md","content":"# Site Plan Facts\n\n# Facts — Valley View Townhomes (SP-2025-0126C)\n**3759 Valley View Road, Austin, TX 78704**\n\n---\n\n## Discrepancies\n\n- **Easement (Greenbelt Access & Use, Instrument 2010070731):** A perpetual, no-build greenbelt easement covering 0.31 acres (13,524 sq ft) of the NE interior of Lot 1 is NOT depicted on the submitted site plan and is NOT in the COA GIS easement layer. County clerk research confirms it is real, recorded, and perpetual. Must be depicted before approval.\n- **Easement (City of Austin Drainage, Instrument 2011021137):** A perpetual City of Austin drainage easement covering 0.130 acres (5,670 sq ft) of the NE corner of Lot 1 is NOT depicted on the submitted site plan and is NOT in the COA GIS easement layer. County clerk research confirms it is real, recorded, and perpetual. Must be depicted before approval.\n- **Combined NE no-build zone (~0.44 ac):** Together, the two easements above encumber approximately 0.44 acres (~19%) of the NE portion of the 2.16-acre site. Neither is shown on submitted plans. Proposed building layout must be evaluated against both easement footprints.\n- **Acreage:** Site plan states 2.16 ac; 2019 deed/survey states 2.158 ac; TCAD states 2.1970 ac; COA GIS parcel measures 2.111 ac (91,944 sq ft). If the controlling acreage is 2.111 ac (GIS), the IC calculation based on 2.16 ac should be rechecked.\n- **10' Wastewater Easement (Vol 73/76, C8S-75-160):** COA GIS shows this plat-dedicated easement running across the parcel. Not confirmed on the submitted site plan. Site plan notes \"must verify no conflicts.\"\n- **25' Drainage Easement and 7.5' PUE (Vol 64/13, C8-71-079):** GIS shows these easements (from the Cortez Heights plat) running across the NE boundary of Lot 1. Not verified on site plan. Precise location relative to proposed improvements must be confirmed.\n- **FIRM Panel Number:** Site plan cites Panel #48453C0585H (effective September 26, 2008). FloodPro returns null for both FIRM_PANEL and EFFECTIVE_DATE. Must be independently verified on FEMA Flood Map Service Center.\n- **Demolition permit number:** Site plan cites \"2022-101266 BP\" as the demolition permit. Amanda shows 2022-101266 as a Plumbing Permit (Repair); the actual demolition building permit is 2022-096813 BP (Final). Actual demolition is confirmed complete.\n\n---\n\n## Property\n\n- **Address:** 3759 Valley View Road, Austin, TX 78704\n- **Parcel ID (GeoID):** 0406100252\n- **Legal Description:** Lot 1, Mecey Subdivision, Volume 73, Page 76, Plat Records of Travis County, Texas\n- **Subdivision:** Mecey Subdivision (File No. C8S-75-100)\n- **Plat Instrument:** Vol 73 / Pg 76 (Instrument 418356), filed 1975-12-11\n- **County:** Travis\n- **Site Area (site plan):** 2.16 acres\n- **Site Area (2019 deed/survey):** 2.158 acres\n- **Site Area (TCAD):** 2.1970 acres (95,701 sq ft)\n- **Site Area (COA GIS parcel):** 2.111 acres (91,944 sq ft)\n- **Limits of Construction:** 2.26 acres\n- **Survey of Record (likely):** 2018 CDS Survey, Project No. 18-03-0225:021\n- **Current Land Use:** Vacant land (formerly assisted living / nursing care facility; demolished June 2022)\n\n## Ownership\n\n- **Owner:** WM 41 Austin RE, LLC\n- **Owner Mailing Address:** 500 West 5th Street, Ste. 1010, Austin, TX 78701\n- **Acquisition Deed:** Instrument 2019197883 — Special Warranty Deed from SHG Resources LP to WM 41 Austin RE LLC (2019-12-11). Property conveyed subject to all easements of record.\n- **Civil Engineering Firm:** Bleyl Engineering\n- **Architecture Firm:** Danze & Davis Architects, Inc.\n- **Landscape Architecture Firm:** Blu Fish Collaborative, Inc.\n\n## Zoning & Land Use\n\n- **Zoning:** MF-2 (Multi-Family Residence — Medium Density)\n- **Controlling Ordinance:** 20120112-092 (Zoning Case C14-2011-0093)\n- **Secondary Ordinance:** 19990225-070b\n- **Neighborhood Plan:** South Lamar NPA — SUSPENDED. No active neighborhood plan applies.\n- **Regulating Plan:** None\n- **Historic District / Landmark:** None\n- **Compatibility Standards:** LDC Subchapter F triggered by adjacent SF-3 parcel (0406100210). Within 25 ft of the SF-3 lot line: maximum 2 stories / 30 ft height. Step-up schedule applies for structures beyond 25 ft. Screening/buffer requirements between MF and SF-3 uses apply.\n- **Nonconforming Signs Area:** Yes (§25-10-152) — all signage must comply with nonconforming signs area standards\n- **Parkland Deficient Area:** Yes (2 polygon features intersect parcel) — parkland dedication fee-in-lieu will apply per unit; confirm fee with Parks Department\n- **Residential Design Standards (Subchapter F):** Active overlay\n\n## Flood\n\n- **FEMA Flood Zone:** Zone X (outside 100-year and 500-year floodplain)\n- **COA Flood Zone:** Zone X\n- **FIRM Panel (site plan claim):** 48453C0585H, effective September 26, 2008 — ⚠️ unverifiable in FloodPro; verify on FEMA portal\n- **FIRM Community:** 480624\n- **Elevation Certificate:** None on file\n- **LOMAs on file:** 0\n- **LOMRs on file:** 0\n- **Floodplain intersection:** None (fema-floodplain layer: empty; fully-developed-floodplain layer: empty)\n\n## Watershed & Environmental\n\n- **Watershed:** West Bouldin Creek (WBO) — Watershed ID 109\n- **Receiving Waters:** Lady Bird Lake → Colorado River below Longhorn Dam\n- **Watershed Class:** Urban\n- **Development Zone:** Desired Development Zone\n- **Impervious Cover Cap:** 65% (LDC §25-8-341)\n- **Proposed Impervious Cover:** ~59.5–60% (under cap — calculated against 2.16 ac site area; see acreage discrepancy)\n- **Existing Impervious Cover:** ~65%\n- **RSMP Participation:** No\n- **Smart Growth Zone:** Yes\n- **Drinking Water Protection Zone:** No\n- **Edwards Aquifer Recharge Zone:** No\n- **Edwards Aquifer Contributing Zone:** No\n- **Creek Buffers:** None intersecting parcel\n- **Salamander Habitat:** No\n- **Underground Storage Tanks:** None registered\n- **Wildland Urban Interface (2024):** No\n\n## Easements & Encumbrances\n\n### ⛔ Critical — Not Depicted on Site Plan, Not in COA GIS\n\n**Easement 1 — Greenbelt Access & Use Easement**\n- **Instrument:** 2010070731 (filed 2010-05-18; executed 2010-03-12)\n- **Grantor:** SHG Resources LP | **Grantee:** Dupont / Barnett / Roberts (assignable to HOA)\n- **Area:** 0.31 acres (13,524 sq ft), NE interior of Lot 1\n- **Duration:** PERPETUAL\n- **Restrictions:** No-build zone. Exclusive pedestrian/greenbelt open space for adjacent condo project. No structures or improvements permitted in easement area.\n- **Status on Site Plan:** Not depicted — must be added before approval\n- **Status in COA GIS:** Not present in easement-polygons layer\n\n**Easement 2 — City of Austin Drainage Easement**\n- **Instrument:** 2011021137 (filed 2011-02-11; executed 2010-03-15)\n- **Grantor:** SHG Resources LP | **Grantee:** City of Austin (Watershed Protection)\n- **Area:** 0.130 acres (5,670 sq ft), NE corner of Lot 1\n- **Duration:** PERPETUAL\n- **Restrictions:** City holds unlimited rights to construct, operate, maintain, replace, upgrade, and repair a drainage channel. No development may occur without City of Austin consent. Related project: Clawson MultiFamily, SP-2010-0014C.SH.\n- **Status on Site Plan:** Not depicted — must be added before approval; requires Watershed Protection coordination\n- **Status in COA GIS:** Not present in easement-polygons layer\n\n### ⚠️ GIS-Confirmed — Not Verified on Site Plan\n\n**Easement 3 — 10' Wastewater Easement**\n- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 73/76, Case C8S-75-160 (Mecey Subdivision plat)\n- **Width:** 10 ft\n- **Status:** Plat-dedicated. In COA GIS. Not confirmed depicted on site plan. Site plan notes \"must verify no conflicts.\"\n\n**Easement 4 — 25' Drainage Easement (from Cortez Heights plat)**\n- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 64/13, Case C8-71-079\n- **Width:** 25 ft\n- **Status:** DEDICATED. Runs across NE boundary of Lot 1. Not verified on site plan.\n\n**Easement 5 — 7.5' Public Utility Easement (from Cortez Heights plat)**\n- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 64/13, Case C8-71-079\n- **Width:** 7.5 ft\n- **Status:** DEDICATED. Runs across NE boundary of Lot 1. Not verified on site plan.\n\n**Easement 6 — 10' Drainage Easement (from Trico Subd plat)**\n- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 69/14, Case C8S-74-075\n- **Width:** 10 ft\n- **Status:** DEDICATED. Runs across NE boundary of Lot 1. Not verified on site plan.\n\n### Plat-Dedicated (Confirm on Survey)\n\n**Easement 7 & 8 — 5' Electric Easements (×2)**\n- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 73/76, Case C8S-75-160\n- **Width:** 5 ft each\n- **Status:** DEDICATED\n\n**Easement 9 — 7.5' Public Utility Easement**\n- **Document Ref:** Travis County Doc 73/76, Case C8S-75-160\n- **Width:** 7.5 ft\n- **Status:** DEDICATED\n\n### Recorded Document Provisions\n\n**Instrument 2010070731 — Greenbelt Access & Use Easement:**\n- Entire 0.31-acre NE interior area is a strict no-build zone; no structures, fences, pavement, or permanent improvements permitted\n- Easement is exclusive to the adjacent condo project beneficiaries and is freely assignable to the project's HOA\n- Runs with the land; binds all successors and assigns of the grantor\n- No time limit; perpetual duration\n- Current property owner (WM 41) took title subject to this easement per the 2019 acquisition deed\n\n**Instrument 2011021137 — City of Austin Drainage Easement:**\n- City of Austin (Watershed Protection Department) holds unlimited, irrevocable right of access to the 0.130-acre NE corner area\n- City may construct, reconstruct, operate, inspect, maintain, repair, replace, and upgrade a drainage channel within the easement\n- Owner may not obstruct City access; any improvements within or adjacent to the easement require City coordination\n- Perpetual; binds all successors and assigns\n\n**Deed Restrictions / Covenants:** None found. County clerk search of 10 strategies found no height limits, use restrictions, parking ratios, setback covenants, or design review obligations recorded against Lot 1.\n\n## Jurisdiction\n\n- **Jurisdiction:** City of Austin — Full Purpose (Code 210)\n- **County:** Travis\n- **Council District:** 5\n- **School District:** Austin ISD (AISD)\n\n## Roadway Classification\n\n**Valley View Road**\n- **ASMP Street Level:** 1 — Local Street\n- **AMTP Classification:** Not in AMTP (local street — not classified as highway, arterial, expressway, or freeway)\n- **Project Type:** Local Mobility\n- **Segment Limits:** Manchaca Rd to End\n- **Existing Lane Count:** Not on file\n- **Future Cross-Section:** 2U-OP (2 undivided lanes, open pavement)\n- **Required ROW:** 58 or 64 ft (determination required — this is commercial redevelopment; single-family exception does not apply)\n- **Required Improvement:** Complete missing sidewalks within existing ROW\n- **Bicycle Facility:** None (existing and recommended)\n- **ROW Dedication Note:** ASMP remarks state ROW dedication may be required from new development and commercial redevelopment through the land development process. Reviewer must determine applicable standard (58 or 64 ft).\n\n## Adjacent Properties\n\n- **SF-3 parcel (0406100210):** Immediate neighbor — triggers LDC Subchapter F compatibility standards. This is the critical adjacency: multi-family development within 25 ft of this SF-3 lot line is subject to 2-story / 30-ft height cap and step-up schedule.\n- **GO parcel (0406100168):** General Office — adjacent\n- **MF-3 parcel (0406100170):** Multi-Family Medium-High Density — adjacent\n- **MF-3-CO parcel (0404090425):** Multi-Family Medium-High Density with Conditional Overlay — adjacent\n- **MF-2-CO parcel (0406101601):** Multi-Family Medium Density with Conditional Overlay — adjacent\n\nThe surrounding context is predominantly multi-family and office, consistent with infill development. The single SF-3 parcel is the critical zoning transition requiring Subchapter F compliance.\n\n## Site Plan Details\n\n- **Site Plan Number:** SP-2025-0126C\n- **Submittal Date:** May 20, 2025\n- **Status:** Awaiting Update (first-round comment response pending)\n- **Fiscal Surety Records:** Filed 2025-09-21 (one for SP-2025-0126C, one for Erosion Controls) — both \"New\" status; suggests progression toward pre-construction conditions\n- **Active Tree Permit:** Commercial Tree Permit pending (filed 2025-08-14)\n\n## Development Specifications\n\n- **Project Name:** Valley View Townhomes\n- **Proposed Use:** 39 multi-family townhome units, 8 buildings\n- **Occupancy Classification:** R3\n- **Construction Type:** Type V-B\n- **Sprinkler System:** NFPA 13D\n- **Proposed Impervious Cover:** ~59.5–60% (reduction from existing ~65%)\n- **Impervious Cover Cap:** 65% (Urban watershed)\n\n## Stormwater\n\n- **Stormwater Feature:** Stacked biofiltration / detention pond (privately maintained)\n- **Watershed:** West Bouldin Creek — Urban, Desired Development Zone\n- **IC Cap:** 65% | Proposed IC: ~59.5–60%\n\n## Fire & Safety\n\n- **Private Hydrants:** 1\n- **Fire Flow Demand:** 3,000 GPM @ 20 psi (reduced to 1,500 GPM with NFPA 13D sprinkler system)\n- **Building Fire Area:** 13,344 sq ft\n- **Wildland Urban Interface:** No\n\n## Development History\n\n- **DA-2021-1307:** Development Assistance, Site Plan Determination/Exemption — Agreed (2021-08-31)\n- **2022-096813 BP:** Building Permit — Demolition — Final (2022-06-28). Existing assisted living building demolished June 2022. Lot currently vacant.\n- **SP-97-0145CS:** Prior site plan — Southwood Nursing Care Center Addition — Approved 1997, Closed 1999 (confirmed prior assisted living use)\n\n","numLines":230,"startLine":1,"totalLines":230}}}
{"level":30,"time":1778189213169,"pid":123,"hostname":"082c71e6-af9","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"13.json","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR25AM2PD4ARWTEXG62BY22A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I have the project context. Key points:\n\n1. **Project:** Valley View Townhomes (SP-2025-0126C), a 39-unit multi-family townhome development in Austin\n2. **Zoning:** MF-2 (Multi-Family Residence — Medium Density)\n3. **Key Regulatory Framework:** \n   - City of Austin Utility Code (UCM) for electrical infrastructure\n   - ECM (Electric Utility Code Manual) Appendix F for utility-compatible trees\n   - Building code/standards referenced as UCM § sections\n   - Critical undisclosed easements in NE area that affect development\n\n4. **Project Details:**\n   - Sheet 21 is the Preliminary Electrical Design Plan (where transformer pads are shown)\n   - 4 transformer pads are proposed\n   - Multiple utilities present: water, wastewater, storm drain, gas, electric\n   - Landscape plan on Sheets 31-32\n   - Fire protection plan on Sheet 10\n   - Grading plan on Sheet 11\n   - Utility tap plan on Sheet 19\n\nNow let me analyze the findings systematically.\n\n**Discipline:** Utility (Electrical Infrastructure) — specifically transformer pad placement, clearances, and associated utility coordination.\n\n**Primary Concern:** Compliance with City of Austin Utility Code (UCM) requirements for pad-mounted transformer location, clearance distances, and access requirements.\n\n**Tier Classification:**\n- **Tier 1** (Within Scope — Electrical discipline owns): All findings about transformer pad clearances to buildings, sidewalks, utilities, vegetation, structural elements. These are electrical/utility compliance requirements.\n- **Tier 2** (Adjacent Scope — cross-department): Easement issues (properties, right-of-way coordination), fire access (fire department), landscape compatibility (Parks/Arborist for tree species).\n- **Tier 3** (Outside Scope): None identified — all findings relate to transformer compliance.\n\n\n- **Findings Organization:** I'm grouping issues into three main categories: missing pad specifications and details, insufficient clearance documentation to adjacent structures, and underdimensioned sidewalk setbacks. The transformer pad detail sheets referenced in EL-13.37 are absent, and critical clearance dimensions to buildings (5-foot minimum per code) aren't shown on the electrical plan. Sidewalk clearance requirements under EL-13.7 also lack proper dimensioning.\n\n- **Access and Vegetation Concerns:** The hot-stick use area around access doors needs verification—EL-13.10 doesn't identify door orientation or confirm the 10-foot clearance zone. Trees and shrubs near transformer locations require utility-compatible species confirmation and root barrier details for plantings within 20 feet, which aren't currently documented across EL-13.13 through EL-13.15.\n\n- **Utility Separation Issues:** Multiple utility lines need minimum 5-foot horizontal clearances from transformer pads, including water and wastewater lines noted in EL-13.21 and EL-13.22, but these separations aren't dimensioned on the plans.\n\n- **Overhead and Underground Coordination:** Gas line clearances (24\" or 36\" depending on pressure), underground utility spacing (12 inches both directions), overhead line clearance (7.5 feet), and distribution radius (15-foot minimum) all require verification through cross-sections showing vertical relationships—EL-13.26 and EL-13.39 currently lack these details.\n\n- **Property Line Verification:** Transformer pad locations need confirmation that they're entirely on private property, which EL-13.33 doesn't establish.\n\n- **Easement Constraints:** EL-13.34 flags potential conflicts with undisclosed easements that could restrict transformer placement; EL-13.36 similarly leaves electrical routing unconfirmed on private land.\n\n- **Parking and Impact Protection:** If transformers sit within 4 feet of parking areas, bollard protection details are missing per EL-13.38.\n\n- **Equipment Specifications:** EL-13.32 doesn't specify whether equipment is oil-filled or dry-type, and drainage direction remains unconfirmed.\n\n- **Structural Clearances:** Retaining walls need dimensioned clearances (5-foot minimum per EL-13.2), and cross-sections showing vertical clearances to overhead structures and parking garages are absent per EL-13.31.\n\n- **Building Feature Separations:** Balconies and overhangs lack documented clearances (EL-13.5), windows/doors/ducts show no clearance details with 12-foot separation required for oil-filled units (EL-13.6), and fence setbacks aren't dimensioned despite varying operator requirements of 3–5 feet (EL-13.3).\n\n- **Emergency Access and Specialized Areas:** Fire exit stairwell clearances aren't dimensioned at 5 feet (EL-13.8); fire escape clearances reference a 20-foot requirement (EL-13.9) despite no fire escapes existing on-site; vertical clearances in niches and enclosed areas lack cross-section documentation (EL-13.11).\n\n- **Site Infrastructure Clearances:** Fire hydrants, bicycle parking, light poles, and fire lanes all lack dimensioned setbacks ranging from 5 to 15 feet (EL-13.12, 13.17, 13.18, 13.19); inter-pad clearances aren't shown (EL-13.20); niche configuration and fire rating/ventilation specifications are missing (EL-13.29, 13.30); reduced 3-foot masonry clearances lack approval (EL-13.4).\n\n- **Transformer Easements:** Easement locations, widths, and AE Design approval are not provided on the plans (EL-13.35).\n\nNow consolidating these 38 findings into roughly 10 comments. The strategy groups related clearance issues together—combining all horizontal clearance deficiencies (16 items) into one comment requiring dimension lines from transformer pads to adjacent features. Transformer pad design specifications form a separate high-severity item blocking approval. Niche details and easement documentation each warrant individual comments due to their distinct requirements. feet)\n      - Gas (24\" or 36\")\n      - Underground utilities (12 inches)\n\nVertical coordination between the pad/lines and other utilities isn't documented, and there's no evidence of clearances to overhead structures or garages. For overhead infrastructure, the design needs to address distribution clearances at 7.5 feet and within a 15-foot radius. Trees selected haven't been verified as utility-compatible per ECM Appendix F, and root barrier specifications are missing along with vegetation separation distances.\n\nSite protection requires bollards where vehicles could impact equipment (4-foot setback minimum), dimensioned fire lane widths of 15 feet, and specific clearances maintained between pads (5 feet), hydrants (5 feet), light poles (5 feet), bike racks (5 feet), and fire exit stairs (5 feet). The transformer pad easements need clarification—easement locations must comply with restrictions, easement boundaries should be explicitly shown, and both pad placement and utility routing require confirmation that they stay within private property limits.\n\nTransformer specifications need to address whether equipment is oil-filled or dry-type, with corresponding drainage requirements, and any specialized configurations like equipment niches should be detailed. These items span multiple departments including Legal/Property and Watershed Protection.\n\nThe clearance requirements are extensive—buildings, balconies, windows/doors/ducts, retaining walls, and fences all have specific minimum distances that aren't currently dimensioned on the plans. For oil-filled transformers, the clearance to openings increases to 12 feet. Any deviations from standard clearances require documented approval.\n\nAccess and operational clearances are missing documentation, including the orientation of access doors, the 10-foot hot-stick clearance zone, vegetation setbacks from both the door and sides of the equipment, and vertical clearance cross-sections. Utility line clearances to the transformer pad also need to be dimensioned and verified against code requirements.\n\nThe coordination between vertical utilities isn't documented—there are no cross-sections showing invert elevations or depths, and it hasn't been confirmed that structures won't be installed beneath AE facilities. The transformer type itself (oil-filled or dry-type) and its drainage slope direction remain unspecified. Additionally, the pads may sit within prohibited easements, and several easement issues need resolution with Property/Legal and Watershed Protection, including confirmation that all pads are entirely on private property and that electrical routing is authorized.\n\nTrees positioned near the pads haven't been verified as utility-compatible per ECM Appendix F standards, and root barrier specifications are missing for any trees within 20 feet—which require a 4-foot depth and 5-foot offset. Site circulation around the transformers also lacks proper documentation: sidewalk setbacks (2 ft minimum from back), bollard placement if within 4 feet of parking, inter-pad spacing (5 ft), fire lane access (15 ft), clearances from light poles (5 ft), fire hydrants (5 ft), bicycle parking (5 ft), and fire exit stairs (5 ft) are all unspecified.\n\nIf a niche configuration is being used, cross-sections demonstrating the 35-foot clearance requirement, 3-hour fire rating certification, and ventilation specifications need to be added. Fire escape clearance doesn't apply since no fire escapes exist on the property.\n\nFor the remaining issues, EL-13.4 addresses reduced 3-foot clearances that lack documentation or approval—this could be consolidated with the building clearance findings or addressed separately. The niche configuration items under EL-13.11 may not be relevant depending on the final design approach. Consolidating these into a streamlined 10-comment structure: transformer pad details and specifications, clearances to buildings and structural elements, access and equipment clearances, and horizontal utility line separation form the core compliance gaps.\n\nVertical utility coordination between installations, transformer equipment type and drainage specifications, property encumbrances and easement disclosures, tree compatibility and root barriers, sidewalk and site circulation protection, and niche service configurations (if required) round out the remaining findings. The fire escape clearance item carries minimal severity since no fire escapes are present on the site. EL-13.29 and 13.30 map to Comment 10, while EL-13.31 belongs to Comment 3. EL-13.32 is addressed in Comment 6, and the cluster of EL-13.33 through 13.36 falls under Comment 7. EL-13.37 corresponds to Comment 1, EL-13.38 to Comment 9, and EL-13.9 stands alone as a separate item with severity 0. This accounts for all findings.\n\nBefore drafting the structured comments, I need to examine each finding more carefully to ensure proper categorization. Looking at the specifics: EL-13.1 concerns transformer pad clearances to buildings where 5 feet is required but not dimensioned, EL-13.2 addresses retaining wall clearances also at 5 feet without dimensions, and EL-13.3 involves fence requirements. ) — not dimensioned\n- **EL-13.20** through **EL-13.25**: Inter-pad, water, wastewater, storm, gas, and underground utility clearances all lack dimensioning, with gas line pressure unspecified and underground utilities missing both horizontal and vertical measurements\n- **EL-13.26**: No cross-installation sections provided for vertical utility coordination\n- **EL-13.27** and **EL-13.28**: Overhead distribution clearances (7.5 ft sky-to-ground) and primary distribution lack dimensioning\n\n, with neutral conductor clearances (15 ft radius) also undocumented\n- **EL-13.29** through **EL-13.32**: Niche service configuration, fire rating (3 hours), ventilation requirements, and equipment type (oil-filled vs dry-type) remain unspecified, with no drainage direction indicated\n- **EL-13.33** through **EL-13.36**: Transformer pads and electrical routing not confirmed as entirely on private property; undisclosed easements present a risk, and transformer easement widths remain undetermined\n- **EL-13.37** and **EL-13.38**: Transformer pad dimensions, AE Design detail sheets, and bollard protection specifications (4-inch diameter if within 4 feet of parking) are missing\n- **EL-13.39**: Vertical clearances to overhead structures lack documentation in cross-sections\n\nI'm organizing these 38 findings into 10 JSON comments with headlines between 3-12 words. Each summary will be roughly 30 words, staying under 50 maximum. The details section preserves all specific information from the findings. Confidence levels correspond to run frequency—3 runs yields confidence 3, 2 runs yields confidence 2, and single runs yield confidence 1. I'm standardizing sheet names and area references, flagging cross-departmental coordination needs, and marking conditional issues or low-confidence items for attention.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"420720f1-c8ef-4630-b41e-df044b1b2b62","uuid":"de4c9618-c419-47f7-8ad9-475766ffebce"}
{"level":30,"time":1778189213170,"pid":123,"hostname":"082c71e6-af9","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"13.json","index":2,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR25AM2PD4ARWTEXG62BY22A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I'll synthesize the electrical infrastructure findings into structured comments. Let me start by analyzing the core patterns and consolidating aggressively.\n\nThe findings are concentrated in transformer pad placement and clearance documentation — a single infrastructure element requiring compliance with City of Austin Utility Code (UCM) § 1.10 and related standards. I have 38 distinct checklist items (some flagged across 3 runs, some across 2, and a few in single runs). Target consolidation: ~10 comments.\n\n**Core Pattern Analysis:**\n\nThe vast majority of findings (30+) concern missing dimensional evidence for various clearances around the four transformer pads. These cluster into logical groups:\n- Horizontal clearances to buildings/structures\n- Horizontal clearances to utilities  \n- Vertical clearances and cross-installation\n- Equipment specifications and site conditions\n- Property encumbrances and easement conflicts\n\nLet me produce the structured output:"}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"420720f1-c8ef-4630-b41e-df044b1b2b62","uuid":"c54ab18c-d7b9-426e-a8e2-41050c9cec7e"}
{"level":30,"time":1778189222910,"pid":123,"hostname":"082c71e6-af9","step":"synthesize-comments","item":"1.json","index":3,"runIndex":"run-1","type":"assistant","message":{"id":"gen_01KR25A30AD8ATHHJCHB73RNBR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a substantial task. I'm being asked to synthesize consolidated findings about overhead electric (OHE) line clearances on a site plan into structured review comments. Let me break down what I need to do:\n\n## Understanding the Input\n\nI have 42 consolidated findings across 3 review runs. Each finding has multiple per-run comments. The findings are all about overhead electric (OHE) clearances and related infrastructure issues.\n\nLooking at the findings:\n- IDs like `1:EL-1.1`, `1:EL-1.2`, etc. (mostly EL- codes, meaning electrical discipline)\n- All findings appear to be flagged as either \"nv\" (not-verifiable) or \"f\" (fail)\n- The issues center on missing clearance dimensions, missing surveyed data, missing elevation sheets, etc.\n- All cite UCM (presumably Austin Utility Code Manual) sections, primarily §1.10.3 and §1.10.6.2\n\n## Discipline Identification\n\nFrom the codes and content, this is clearly **Utility (Electrical)** discipline. The primary concerns are:\n- Clearances from overhead electric lines to buildings, structures, driveways, etc.\n- Surveyed conductor elevation data\n- Dimension annotations on plans\n- Compliance with UCM clearance standards\n- Construction staging safety near energized lines\n\n## Scope Classification\n\nAll these findings are **Tier 1 — Within Scope**. They all directly relate to electrical infrastructure clearance requirements that the utility/electrical discipline owns. Some have adjacent concerns (e.g., construction staging involves safety), but the core issue is electrical clearance compliance.\n\n## Consolidation Strategy\n\nI have 42 input findings. My target is roughly 1 comment per 4 findings, so I should produce about 10-11 consolidated comments.\n\nLooking at the findings, I can group them by theme:\n1. **Missing horizontal clearance dimensions** (EL-1.1, EL-1.2, EL-1.9, EL-1.14, EL-1.26, EL-1.27, EL-1.36, EL-1.43) — specific to buildings, fences, retaining walls, trash enclosures, transformers, lighting\n\n\n2. **Missing building elevation sheets** (EL-1.7, EL-1.3) — lack of vertical clearance verification\n3. **Missing section/detail views** (EL-1.8, EL-1.17) — plan and elevation exhibits with dimension lines\n4. **Missing surveyed conductor data** (EL-1.31, EL-1.1, EL-1.6) — registered surveyor certification\n5. **Driveway vertical clearances** (EL-1.18, EL-1.19) — driveways crossing overhead lines\n6. **Missing clearance dimensions overall** (EL-1.23) — fundamental documentation\n7. **Pole-to-feature distances** (EL-1.10, EL-1.22, EL-1.25) — fences and driveways positioned relative to poles\n8. **Utility-compatible plantings** (EL-1.37) — tree species selection near energized conductors\n9. **Notification requirements** (EL-1.46) — 48-hour advance notice not documented\n10. **Guy wire and anchor conflicts** (EL-1.40, EL-1.42) — easement boundaries and site feature interference\n11. **Water service routing** (EL-1.28, EL-1.29) — meter and line separation from electrical infrastructure\n12. **Equipment clearance during construction** (EL-1.45) — staging areas and machinery proximity to live lines\n13. **Roof and overhang projections** (EL-1.3, EL-1.6) — building envelope encroachments\n14. **Meter clearance and access** (EL-1.meter clearances** (EL-1.38) — working space around meters\n15. **Driveway/parking structure issues** (EL-1.5, EL-1.13, EL-1.19, EL-1.22, EL-1.24, EL-1.25, EL-1.32, EL-1.35, EL-1.39, EL-1.47) — various site features\n16. **Transformer clearances** (EL-1.43)\n17. **Service mast clearances** (EL-1.47)\n18. **Not applicable** (EL-1.4) — no gas pumps on site\n\nThe core issue emerging is a pattern of missing horizontal clearance dimensions. The 7.5-foot sky-to-ground requirement appears across multiple building elements—the main structure itself (EL-1.1 with three separate violations), building projections like overhangs (EL-1.3 with two instances), and ground-level features including driveways and pedestrian areas (EL-1.5). This suggests the site plan lacks consistent dimensional documentation for vertical clearance verification.\n\nA second major gap involves radial clearance verification. Buildings need to demonstrate the 15-foot radial clearance from conductors (EL-1.2 with three instances), and this requirement extends to projections and driveway-related structures like posts or barriers (EL-1.13). The pattern indicates missing calculations or survey data confirming these distances.\n\nThe third critical deficiency is the absence of building elevation documentation. No elevation sheets showing actual surveyed conductor heights exist (EL-1.7 with three occurrences), and there are no section or elevation details with dimension lines to support clearance claims (EL-1.8 with three instances). Additionally, surveyed clearance data for buildings and driveways is missing entirely (EL-1.31 with three runs, EL-1.6 with two runs).\n\nDriveway vertical clearance under overhead lines also lacks documentation. The minimum 16–18 feet requirement for driveways (EL-1.18 with three runs) and parking areas (EL-1.19) needs to be shown with supporting dimensions.\n\nA fundamental documentation failure stands out: no dimensions from the outer conductor appear on any sheet (EL-1.23 with three runs). This represents a core gap requiring immediate attention.\n\nPole and guy wire issues form another cluster. Fence posts need 10-foot clearance from poles (EL-1.10 with three runs), driveways need 30-foot clearance (EL-1.22 with two runs), and guy wire anchors show potential conflicts (EL-1.40 and EL-1.42 with two runs each). Sidewalk clearances from poles (EL-1.25), pole crossing angles (EL-1.32 with two runs), service mast clearances (EL-1.47), and potential new pole locations for underground conversion (EL-1.35) also need resolution.\n\nWater meter and water line clearances from service drops and distribution facilities require verification (EL-1.28 and EL-1.29 with two runs each), along with building foundation and utility access clearances (EL-1.30 with two runs). Tree species within 10 feet of conductors haven't been verified as utility-compatible (EL-1.37 with three runs). Retaining wall section exhibits with clearance dimensions are missing (EL-1.17 with three runs). Construction staging plans with clearances and the required 48-hour notification note are absent (EL-1.45 and EL-1.46 with two and three runs respectively). Meter area details showing required working space clearances also need to be added (EL-1.38 with two runs). Driveway vertical clearance documentation for overhead line crossings (EL-1.18) and parking areas (EL-1.19) needs to be provided. The design lacks any conductor-to-outer-edge dimensions across the sheets (EL-1.23), which is fundamental to clearance verification. Pole placement creates several conflicts: insufficient setbacks from fences (EL-1.10) and driveways (EL-1.22), guy wire anchor easement requirements (EL-1.42), and potential interference with sidewalks (EL-1.25). Guy wire routing also conflicts with existing site features (EL-1.40), and pole crossing angles require clarification (EL-1.32). Underground service conversion and transformer placement issues also need resolution. Looking at the retaining wall issues, EL-1.14 and EL-1.17 both address clearance documentation but focus on different aspects—one on horizontal dimensions and the other on section details. These should be combined into a single comment about retaining wall clearance gaps rather than split across multiple themes. The consolidation approach makes sense: grouping the horizontal clearance dimension issue with the missing section exhibit details creates one cohesive comment addressing the complete documentation deficiency for that element. EL-1.18 (driveways crossing overhead lines lack vertical clearance dimensions), EL-1.19 (parking areas lack vertical clearance dimensions). There's also a fundamental gap where no clearance dimensions from the outer conductor appear anywhere on the plan sheets (EL-1.23). Retaining walls are missing both horizontal clearance dimensions and section exhibits showing clearances (EL-1.14, EL-1.17). Pole-related issues span fence clearances, driveway proximity, sidewalk setbacks, crossing angles, and guy wire conflicts with site features plus missing anchor easements (EL-1.10, EL-1.22, EL-1.25, EL-1.32, EL-1.40, EL-1.42). Service infrastructure clearances need documentation for underground conversion poles, transformers, and service masts (EL-1.35, EL-1.43, EL-1.47). Water system and utility clearances are missing for meters relative to service drops, water lines themselves, and building foundation access points (EL-1.28, EL-1.29, EL-1.30). Trees near overhead lines haven't been verified as utility-compatible species (EL-1.37). Construction safety documentation is incomplete—missing the 48-hour notification requirement and a staging plan that shows clearances during work (EL-1.46, EL-1.45).\n\nI'm also noticing some findings I haven't yet captured: EL-1.24 and EL-1.39 both concern streetlight pole locations not being verified against overhead lines, and EL-1.27 involves electric easements recorded at 5 feet but lacking clearance dimension annotations showing the required 7.5-foot distance from the outside conductor. Let me verify the complete count against the raw data to ensure nothing's been missed.\n\nLooking at the frequency distribution, several items appear multiple times across different runs—EL-1.1, EL-1.2, EL-1.7, EL-1.8, EL-1.9, EL-1.10, EL-1.14, EL-1.17, EL-1.18, EL-1.23, EL-1.31, EL-1.37, and EL-1.46 each show up 3 times, while others like EL-1.3, EL-1.6, EL-1.22, EL-1.28, EL-1.29, EL-1.38, EL-1.40, EL-1.42, and EL-1.45 appear twice, with some findings occurring just once.\n\nAcross all runs, I'm working with 40 unique findings total, though EL-1.4 doesn't apply here since there are no gas pumps on site. That leaves me with 39 Tier 1 findings to consolidate and organize.\n\nLooking at what I haven't yet addressed, several findings cluster around pole placement and easement clearances: the photometric plan lacks streetlight pole clearance verification, electric easements need documented 7.5-foot conductor clearances, and survey drawings don't clearly dimension the streetlight poles themselves. I should group these together—fence posts, driveway edges, sidewalk proximity, and easement dimensions all relate to how poles interact with site features and utility corridors. EL-1.27 is fundamentally about demonstrating clearance dimensions within the easement zone rather than pole placement itself. The core issue—missing annotations showing 7.5-foot horizontal clearances—aligns with the broader clearance dimension problem across the site.\n\nMerging EL-1.27 into the horizontal clearance comment makes sense since it addresses the same requirement, just applied specifically to easement areas. This consolidates related findings while maintaining the distinction that easements require particular attention. The revised grouping becomes: horizontal clearances (EL-1.1, EL-1.3, EL-1.5, EL-1.9, EL-1.16, EL-1.26, EL-1.27, EL-1.28, EL-1.36), radial clearances (EL-1.2, EL-1.5, EL-1.13), and building elevation documentation.\n\nThe remaining comments address: conductor data gaps, driveway clearances, outer conductor dimensions, retaining wall clearances, pole and easement conflicts, service infrastructure, water meter clearances from drops, water line routing, tree species verification, and construction safety protocols. EL-1.28 warrants separation from general water line issues since it specifically concerns meter clearances from service drops rather than line routing itself.\n\nConsolidating water utilities back together—meters, lines, and associated clearances under one comment—reduces the total to 11 distinct issues. Now verifying coverage: EL-1.5 appears across multiple comments due to its dual relevance to both 7.5-foot and radial clearance requirements, which needs clarification on whether it should be listed once or multiple times depending on the categorization approach.\n\nScanning through the full list of 39 findings, most map cleanly to their assigned comments. However, EL-1.38 (meter working space clearances) stands out as unassigned—it addresses access requirements around meter locations rather than overhead line clearances specifically. This could either warrant its own comment or integrate with the broader service infrastructure grouping, depending on whether the focus is strictly overhead clearances or includes all utility access issues. Merging the meter working space issue into Comment 8 makes sense since both address electrical service infrastructure gaps. Renaming it to cover meters, transformers, and masts consolidates related findings—EL-1.35, EL-1.38, EL-1.43, EL-1.47—bringing the total to 12 comments across 39 findings, which maintains a reasonable 3.25:1 ratio.\n\nI'm also considering whether organizing by what's actually missing—rather than by component type—might be more effective. For instance, grouping findings around missing horizontal sky-to-ground clearance dimensions (the 7.5-foot requirement) would capture EL-1.1, EL-1.3, EL-1.5, EL-1.9, EL-1.16, and others that all lack proper dimension annotations on the plans. **Construction Safety & Compliance Documentation Missing** — notification and staging requirements not documented. Details: EL-1.46, EL-1.45\n\nThe consolidation ratio of roughly 3.5:1 across 11 comments for 39 findings appears appropriate. Now structuring each comment requires identifying the applicable area—considering whether to use canonical zones like \"Site Interior,\" \"Building Envelope,\" \"Plan Documentation,\" or \"Utilities & Infrastructure\" depending on the scope of each finding and whether it's localized to specific features or distributed across the site.\n\nFor the horizontal sky-to-ground clearance dimensions issue affecting overhead electric facilities, \"Utilities & Infrastructure\" better captures the nature of the documentation gap than a site-wide designation, since it specifically addresses utility clearance requirements. The sheets involved span multiple plan sets: consolidating references from EL-1.1 (sheets 4, 9, 11), EL-1.3 (sheets 9, 11, 21), EL-1.5 (sheets 9, 11, 22), and EL-1.9 (sheets 9, 31) to identify which plan sheets require updates. Clearance verification is mandated by code (UCM § 1.10.3), and without these dimensions documented, I can't verify compliance. This blocks approval, making it severity 3.\n\nThe pattern across runs shows consistent flagging—EL-1.1 and EL-1.9 each appeared in 3 runs, while most others appeared 1-2 times, averaging about 1.8 occurrences per finding. This consistency reinforces the reliability of the issue. Citation: \"UCM § 1.10.3\"\nConfidence: 3 (all 3 runs flagged)\n\nBuilding projections like overhangs and balconies lack documented clearances from overhead conductors, making it impossible to confirm they stay within the required 7.5-foot sky-to-ground envelope. Driveways and pedestrian walkways similarly need section and plan views with explicit clearance dimensions from electric distribution lines, but these exhibits are absent from the submission. Site features and appurtenances also require dimensioned clearance documentation to demonstrate compliance with the overhead conductor setback requirements. from overhead electric facilities when trash bins are raised during service. Electric easements shown on the plat and grading plan lack dimension annotations confirming items within them maintain the required 7.5-foot clearance from the outside conductor. Water meter locations on the electrical plan similarly need clearance dimension annotations for the 7.5-foot horizontal sky-to-ground clearance from overhead electric service-drop conductors.\n\nDecorative and site lighting fixtures appear on photometric and landscape plans but lack clearance verification dimensions showing the 7.5-foot sky-to-ground clearances from overhead electric lines. I need to add runComments arrays for each detail with the per-run flagging data, pulling from the source data structure where each run provides specific observations about what's missing or needs verification. the distance from the outside conductor to building walls, compliance with the 7.5-foot minimum horizontal clearance cannot be verified. The review identifies that while overhead electric lines are documented on the site and landscape plans with utility poles marked, the critical dimension annotations showing clearance distances from the outside conductor to building faces are absent. Buildings 7 and 8 are noted as the structures in closest proximity to the overhead line, making this documentation gap particularly significant for code compliance verification.","signature":""}],"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a7fd24c-676b-498c-9f16-8216c5e40215","uuid":"82f4b304-706e-407a-9489-7290dd84e973"}
